Member layout by search over a model of C++ types
=================================================

This example lays out a struct and searches for the member order of the
smallest size.  It closes the series of four that opens with
20-cxx-types-deduction.  The README of that example describes the shared
model under ../cxx and the motivation, a future integration with the Circle
C++ compiler.  Python describes the struct, as a reflection would, and Curry
lays it out and searches.

The layout follows the Itanium C++ ABI for a standard-layout struct without
bit-fields, base classes or virtual members.  Each member goes to the next
offset that is a multiple of its alignment.  The alignment of the struct is
the largest alignment of a member.  The size is the end of the last member
rounded up to that alignment.  The sizes and alignments come from the LP64
data model of CxxLimits.  The model is a simplification of the C++ type
system: it has no bit-fields, no bases, no virtual members, no nested class
members, no alignas and no packing pragmas.

The module CxxLayout.curry under ../cxx holds the layout and the search.
This directory holds the driver only.


1. What the example shows about functional logic programming
-------------------------------------------------------------

Non-determinism as values.  The function perm builds one order of the
members with insert.  Both rules of insert apply to a non-empty list, so each
call is a choice.  An expression over perm means every order at once:

    sizeOfOrder ss = (structSize (layoutSized order), order)
      where order = perm ss

One value of sizeOfOrder is the size of one order, paired with that order.
The function has 720 values for six members.

Set functions.  The set function set1 sizeOfOrder is the set of every such
pair.  The search sorts the set by size, counts the run of the smallest size,
and takes the first order of that run in the order of the member names:

    bySize = sortValuesBy (\p q -> fst p <= fst q) pairs
    best   = [order | (size, order) <- bySize, size == fst (head bySize)]
    result = (length bySize, length best, layoutSized (minimum best))

The sort compares sizes only.  The scheduler does not promise an order of the
values across the backends, so minimum picks the order to print.  The names
are unique, and minimum compares them first.

Constraints as rules.  Scenario (c) keeps the member tag first.  The search
is the same with one more rule, a guard on the generated order:

    sizeOfOrderFirst first ss | nameOf (head order) == first = (size, order)
      where (size, order) = sizeOfOrder ss

An order with another member in front fails.  A failure is not a value, so
the set function does not see it, and the set shrinks from 720 values to
120.  The guard needs only the head of the order.  Laziness builds the head
first, so an order that breaks the rule fails before the rest of it is built.

In this struct the pin costs no bytes.  The members urgent, port and length
fill the eight bytes behind tag, and both searches reach 24 bytes.  The pin
costs orders: 2 of 120 reach 24 bytes, against 48 of 720.


2. What the example shows about calling Curry from Python
----------------------------------------------------------

The driver go.py adds ../cxx to curry.path and imports CxxLimits, CxxType and
CxxLayout from curry.lib.  The struct is a Python class whose annotations are
its members:

    class Packet:
      tag: CHAR
      stamp: DOUBLE
      ...

The dictionary Packet.__annotations__ keeps the declaration order.  That is
the reflection of Python, and it stands in for the reflection of a compiler:
Circle would hand over the members of the struct under compilation in the
same shape.  Each type is a Curry expression built with curry.expr from the
constructors of CxxType, for example

    curry.expr(CxxType.Ptr, curry.expr(CxxType.Const, CHAR))

for char const*.  The function members turns the annotations into a Python
list of (name, type) pairs, and curry.eval converts it to a Curry list of
tuples.  A name is passed as a list of characters, because a str of length
one would become a Char.

A layout comes back as nested tuples and lists.  The call

    curry.eval(CxxLayout.layout, members, converter='topython')

yields a Python tuple of the placed members, each a tuple of name, offset,
size and padding, then the size, the alignment and the padding at the end.
Python prints the table.  The type of each member goes through the Curry
printer, curry.eval(CxxType.showCxx, t), so Python holds no knowledge of C++
syntax.  The layout also goes back to Curry, to CxxLayout.padding, so the
tuple converts both ways.  The names travel inside the tuples on purpose: a
bare list of Curry strings comes back as one str, because the converter
joins a list of strings.


3. How to run
-------------

Run "make stage" from the root of the repository first.  Then run

    ./run

The run script uses the C++ backend by default.  To pick a backend, set
SPRITE_INTERPRETER_FLAGS in the environment:

    SPRITE_INTERPRETER_FLAGS=backend:py ./run
    SPRITE_INTERPRETER_FLAGS=backend:cxx ./run

The first run also compiles CxxLayout.curry, and the two shared modules when
no earlier example of the series has run.  The build products go to .curry/
next to each source.

To lay out another struct, edit the class Packet in go.py.  The member types
may be fundamental types, pointers, references, arrays and pointers to
members.  A member of a class type has no size in the model, so a struct with
one has no layout, and the driver stops with StopIteration.


4. Expected output
------------------

The file expected.out holds the exact output of ./run.  Scenario (a) prints
the struct in declaration order, one row per member and one per hole:

    (a) struct Packet in declaration order
      offset  size  member
           0     1  char         tag
           1     7  (padding)
           8     8  double       stamp
          16     1  bool         urgent
          17     3  (padding)
          20     4  int          length
          24     2  short        port
          26     6  (padding)
          32     8  char const*  payload
      sizeof 40, alignof 8, padding 16

Scenario (b) searches every order:

    (b) the order of the smallest size
      smallest sizeof 24, reached by 48 of 720 orders
      the first of those orders: length, port, tag, urgent, payload, stamp

Scenario (c) searches the orders with tag first:

    (c) the order of the smallest size with tag first
      smallest sizeof 24, reached by 2 of 120 orders
      the first of those orders: tag, urgent, port, length, payload, stamp

The table of the chosen order follows each summary.  The output is identical
on both backends.


5. Limits
---------

A warm run of ./run took 0.6 s on the C++ backend and 15 s on the Python
backend.  The first run also compiles CxxLayout.curry.  It took 2.6 s on the
C++ backend and 16 s on the Python backend, with the shared modules compiled
by an earlier example.

The number of orders is the factorial of the number of members.  A seventh
member makes 5040 orders.  On the C++ backend the two searches then took
4.4 s and 0.6 s.  On the Python backend the search with the pin took 20 s,
and the search over every order hit the recursion limit of Python after
200 s, in the sort of 5040 pairs.  On that backend a sort of 1000 elements
works, a sort of 2000 and the length of a list of 5040 hit the same limit.
Keep the Python backend at six members.

The layout is the layout of a standard-layout struct.  A struct with a base
class, a virtual function or a bit-field has another layout, and the model
does not describe it.
